<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="<%= theme.followingSystem ? 'system' : theme.id %>">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Answers - forgeright</title>
    <%- include('partials/theme-head', { pageStyles: ['console'] }) %>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">
    <%- include('partials/demo-banner') %>
    <header>
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">forgeright</p>
            <h1><%= organisation.name %></h1>
        </div>
        <div class="switchers">
            <%- include('partials/theme-picker') %>
            <form method="post" action="<%= basePath %>/admin/logout">
                <button type="submit" class="secondary">Sign out</button>
            </form>
        </div>
    </header>

    <%- include('partials/console-nav', { activeView: 'answers' }) %>

    <%- include('partials/console-notice', { subject: 'The answer' }) %>

    <p class="intro">
        Every answer, as the participant gave it. Open one to see which process it was
        matched to and why, and the score that process carries. The paragraph is checked
        against these words, not the other way round.
    </p>

    <% if (responses.length === 0) { %>
        <div class="card"><p class="empty">No one has answered yet.</p></div>
    <% } %>

    <% /* A response is the subject here: "this answer was read wrong". */ %>
    <% responses.forEach(function (response) { %>
        <details class="item">
            <summary>
                <span class="summary-main">
                    <%#
                        THE SHAPE IS RESOLVED, NOT ASSUMED. This read `response.data.task`
                        directly, which is undefined for every INTERVIEW round - those save
                        `{ workflows: [...] }`, while only an imported round has `task` at the
                        top level. So every live interview was titled "Untitled answer".
                        `responseTitle` reads whichever shape arrived; see lib/view-format.js.
                    %>
                    <strong><%= responseTitle(response.data) %></strong>
                    <div class="meta">
                        <%= response.department %>
                        &middot; answered <%= shortInstant(response.created_at) %>
                    </div>
                </span>
                <span class="summary-side">
                    <%#
                        STILL IN PROGRESS. A round with no `submitted_at` is one the
                        participant has not finished: they answered some questions and
                        may come back. It is shown because their words are worth reading,
                        and it contributes nothing to the roadmap, so the tag is what tells
                        an admin which is which.

                        THE CALM REGISTER, matching `.review` rather than the warning
                        yellow: this is a fact about the answer, not something wrong with
                        it. An abandoned interview is ordinary - it is the reason
                        progressive saving exists - so the label informs rather than
                        alarms. The base `.tag` styling is the neutral idle colour.

                        IT RENDERS BEFORE THE GROUPING TAG, because it is about the answer
                        itself rather than about how it was read, and a partial has no
                        grouping to describe.
                    %>
                    <% if (response.submitted_at === null) { %>
                        <span class="tag" title="The participant has not finished this round. It is kept and readable, and it is left out of the roadmap's sorting and scoring until it is completed.">still in progress</span>
                    <% } else if (response.empty) { %>
                        <%#
                            A FINISHED ROUND WITH NOTHING IN IT, which is a state the console
                            could not previously see: `submitted_at` IS set, so the "still in
                            progress" tag does not apply, and the row rendered exactly like a
                            completed interview - `Untitled answer`, no fields, and a Rejudge
                            button promising a result the data cannot give.

                            IT IS DERIVED, not stored: no field carries evidence, which means
                            every one was declined. The message says what happened rather than
                            presenting it as a failure, because a participant who could not put
                            the work into words has told the reader something true.
                        %>
                        <span class="tag" title="Every question was asked and none was answered, so this round holds no evidence. Nothing was scored from it, and there is nothing to rejudge.">nothing answered</span>
                    <% } %>
                    <% if (response.grouping) { %>
                        <span class="tag<%= response.grouping.decidedBy === 'admin' ? ' departments' : '' %>">
                            <%
                                // The tag names whether a person asked for this grouping,
                                // so an admin can see which results came from a rejudge.
                            %><%= response.grouping.decidedBy === 'admin' ? 'regrouped' : 'grouped' %>
                        </span>
                        <%# THE SAME FLAG AS THE ROADMAP, per answer, because this is where
                            an admin reads one response and its grouping. Quiet by design:
                            see the note on the roadmap's tag and in console.css. %>
                        <% if (response.grouping.needsReview) { %>
                            <span class="tag review" title="<%= response.grouping.needsReviewReason %>">split while unsure</span>
                        <% } %>
                    <% } else { %>
                        <span class="tag">not yet read</span>
                    <% } %>
                </span>
            </summary>

            <div class="detail">
                <h3>What the participant said</h3>
                <% /* The participant's own words, which the paragraph is checked against. */ %>
                <%# The RESOLVED workflow, so the partial reads fields from the object that
                    actually holds them. It already handles a null by saying the answer has no
                    stored fields, which is the honest thing for a row with neither shape. %>
                <%- include('partials/response-fields', { data: responseWorkflow(response.data) }) %>

                <% if (response.grouping) { %>
                    <h3>Read as this process</h3>
                    <p class="who"><%= response.grouping.processName %></p>
                    <p class="meta">
                        <%= response.grouping.confidence %> confidence
                        &middot; <%= response.grouping.decidedBy %>
                        &middot; <%= response.grouping.decidedAt %>
                        &middot; contributed: <%= response.grouping.contributed.join(', ') || 'not recorded' %>
                    </p>

                    <h3>Why it was matched there</h3>
                    <% /* The model's reasoning, shown because the spec makes it the only way
                        a grouping can be reviewed. Escaped like every other value. */ %>
                    <p class="paragraph"><%= response.grouping.reasoning %></p>
                <% } else { %>
                    <p class="empty">This answer has not been identified into a process yet.</p>
                <% } %>

                <%#
                    THE RAW CONVERSATION, IN A COLLAPSED DETAIL RATHER THAN OPEN.

                    IT EXISTS SO AN ANSWER CAN BE READ AGAINST ITS QUESTION: "eight minutes"
                    means one thing asked about duration and another about the count, and the
                    submitted fields alone cannot settle that.

                    COLLAPSED BECAUSE IT IS THE LONGEST CONTENT IN THE SYSTEM. A full interview
                    is a dozen or more turns, and rendering it open would push every other
                    answer off the page and make the answers view read as a transcript dump
                    rather than a summary. The `<summary>` names what is inside and gives the
                    turn count, so a reader knows it is there without opening every answer to
                    find out - which was the previous problem: the block existed and nothing
                    hinted at it.

                    A NESTED `<details>`, not a heading, because this sits INSIDE the answer's
                    own collapsed detail: one click opens the answer, two open the raw words.

                    ESCAPED WITH THE ORDINARY TAG. This is the largest block of participant
                    text on any page, so it is also where an escaping mistake would matter
                    most. The stored shape is `{ role, content }` per turn, validated at the
                    boundary in lib/auth/participant-routes.js.

                    THE FORMAT-COMMITMENT TURN CANNOT APPEAR HERE. It is added by
                    `withFormatCommitment` at the PROVIDER call, server-side, after the
                    browser's conversation has already been sent; the transcript stored is the
                    browser's own `state.conversation`, so the synthetic turn was never in it.
                %>
                <% if (response.transcript && response.transcript.length > 0) { %>
                    <details class="transcript">
                        <summary>
                            The conversation it came from
                            <span class="meta"><%= response.transcript.length %> turns</span>
                        </summary>
                        <% response.transcript.forEach(function (turn) { %>
                            <p class="words transcript-turn<%= turn.role === 'assistant' ? ' analyst' : '' %>">
                                <strong><%= turn.role === 'assistant' ? 'Analyst' : 'Participant' %>:</strong>
                                <%= turn.content %>
                            </p>
                        <% }); %>
                    </details>
                <% } %>

                <%#
                    NO REJUDGE FORM FOR A PARTIAL ROUND, and this is a fix rather than a
                    preference. The form used to render for every row, including a round
                    the participant has not finished - while the route behind it reads the
                    response through `getResponsesForAnalysis`, which EXCLUDES partials.
                    So submitting it answered "that response is not in this organisation",
                    offering an action that could only fail.

                    The rule and the button now agree: a partial is unfinished work, it is
                    excluded from sorting and scoring, and identifying it is the same
                    decision seen from the other side. A round that is not finished has no
                    grouping to dispute yet.
                %>
                <% if (response.submitted_at !== null && !response.empty) { %>
                <% /* A rejudge is a request with a reason, never a category to pick. */ %>
                <form class="rejudge" method="post" action="<%= basePath %>/admin/answers/<%= response.id %>/rejudge">
                    <label for="reason-<%= response.id %>">This answer was read wrong</label>
                    <textarea id="reason-<%= response.id %>" name="reason" required
                              placeholder="Say why the grouping looks wrong. The tool re-identifies with your reasoning, and is free to disagree."></textarea>
                    <button type="submit"<%= readOnly ? ' disabled' : '' %> class="secondary">Rejudge this answer</button>
                </form>
                <% } else if (response.empty) { %>
                    <p class="meta">
                        Every question was asked and none was answered, so there is nothing here to
                        group, score or rejudge. The round is kept because "we asked and nobody
                        could say" is itself worth knowing.
                    </p>
                <% } else { %>
                    <p class="meta">
                        This round is still in progress, so it is not grouped or scored. Rejudging
                        becomes possible once the participant finishes it.
                    </p>
                <% } %>
            </div>
        </details>
    <% }); %>
</div>
</body>
</html>
